{"reason": "The execution results show the model reaches a validation log loss of 0.1006 and then plateaus, aligning with the initial plan's observation. The initial plan's root cause analysis (only top-layer features, uniform training) is valid. Similar historical experiments successfully used multi-scale feature fusion and progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rates. Therefore, I focus on model_design (to replace simple CLS concatenation with multi-scale feature fusion from last 4 DeBERTa layers) and training_evaluation (to implement progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rate decay). These modules directly address the identified limitations and leverage proven strategies from historical experiments.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current simple concatenation of the CLS token and auxiliary features with a multi-scale feature fusion module that takes hidden states from the last 4 layers of DeBERTa, applies learned per-layer attention weights, and fuses them with auxiliary features via a gated cross-attention mechanism. This captures hierarchical stylistic patterns (syntax, vocabulary, sentence structure) better than a single CLS token. Use a lightweight cross-attention layer with 4 heads and output dimension 256. The auxiliary features (from punctuation and vocabulary detectors) are projected to 256 and fused via gating. Preserve the existing backbone, auxiliary encoders, and classifier interface; only modify how the transformer hidden states are combined. The number of trainable parameters should remain similar (increase by ~2M).", "training_evaluation": "Implement progressive unfreezing schedule: start with only the last layer (layer 11) unfrozen, then unfreeze layer 10 at epoch 3, layer 9 at epoch 6, layer 8 at epoch 9, and fully unfreeze all 4 layers at epoch 12. Use a layer-wise learning rate multiplier of 0.95 per layer (starting lr=2e-5 for layer 11, 1.9e-5 for layer 10, etc.) while keeping auxiliary component lr at 1e-4 and classifier head lr at 5e-5. Apply cosine annealing with warm restarts (T0=total_steps/3). Remove warmup step since progressive unfreezing provides gradual adaptation. Keep early stopping patience of 5 and max 30 epochs. This schedule prevents catastrophic forgetting and allows lower layers to specialize in author-specific patterns, as suggested by the initial plan and similar successful experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show the model reaches a validation log loss of 0.1006 and then plateaus, aligning with the initial plan's observation. The initial plan's root cause analysis (only top-layer features, uniform training) is valid. Similar historical experiments successfully used multi-scale feature fusion and progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rates. Therefore, I focus on model_design (to replace simple CLS concatenation with multi-scale feature fusion from last 4 DeBERTa layers) and training_evaluation (to implement progressive unfreezing with layer-wise learning rate decay). These modules directly address the identified limitations and leverage proven strategies from historical experiments.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current simple concatenation of the CLS token and auxiliary features with a multi-scale feature fusion module that takes hidden states from the last 4 layers of DeBERTa, applies learned per-layer attention weights, and fuses them with auxiliary features via a gated cross-attention mechanism. This captures hierarchical stylistic patterns (syntax, vocabulary, sentence structure) better than a single CLS token. Use a lightweight cross-attention layer with 4 heads and output dimension 256. The auxiliary features (from punctuation and vocabulary detectors) are projected to 256 and fused via gating. Preserve the existing backbone, auxiliary encoders, and classifier interface; only modify how the transformer hidden states are combined. The number of trainable parameters should remain similar (increase by ~2M).",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement progressive unfreezing schedule: start with only the last layer (layer 11) unfrozen, then unfreeze layer 10 at epoch 3, layer 9 at epoch 6, layer 8 at epoch 9, and fully unfreeze all 4 layers at epoch 12. Use a layer-wise learning rate multiplier of 0.95 per layer (starting lr=2e-5 for layer 11, 1.9e-5 for layer 10, etc.) while keeping auxiliary component lr at 1e-4 and classifier head lr at 5e-5. Apply cosine annealing with warm restarts (T0=total_steps/3). Remove warmup step since progressive unfreezing provides gradual adaptation. Keep early stopping patience of 5 and max 30 epochs. This schedule prevents catastrophic forgetting and allows lower layers to specialize in author-specific patterns, as suggested by the initial plan and similar successful experiments."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows validation logloss plateauing at 0.1466 with learning rate stuck at 1.71e-05, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis of underfitting and incorrect scheduler. Similar successful experiments used LoRA for efficient full-layer fine-tuning and OneCycleLR scheduler to break optimization plateaus, while failed experiments showed that complex multi-scale fusion and progressive unfreezing (currently used) lead to diminishing returns. Therefore, I focus on model_design (replace progressive unfreezing with LoRA) and training_evaluation (use OneCycleLR and Focal Loss) to directly address root causes without deviating from the initial plan's direction. Data augmentation is kept but simplified (only synonym replacement, no punctuation swapping) based on successful historical setups.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current progressive unfreezing and multi-scale fusion (including PunctuationPatternEncoder, ArchaicVocabularyDetector, LayerAttention, and GatedCrossAttention) with LoRA applied to all query/value projection matrices in all 24 layers of DeBERTa-v3-large (rank=8, alpha=16, dropout=0.1). Keep only the classification head and remove all auxiliary modules to avoid overfitting. This allows simultaneous all-layer adaptation with only ~2M trainable parameters, directly addressing the underfitting caused by frozen backbone layers.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current cosine annealing scheduler (stuck at 1.71e-05) with OneCycleLR (max_lr=5e-4 for LoRA params, 1e-3 for head, pct_start=0.1, anneal_strategy='cos'), and replace label smoothing cross-entropy with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=class-balanced). Keep data augmentation as synonym replacement (30% of content words via WordNet) applied inside __getitem__. This provides proper learning rate dynamics and focuses on hard examples, breaking the validation plateau."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows validation logloss plateauing at 0.1466 with learning rate stuck at 1.71e-05, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis of underfitting and incorrect scheduler. Similar successful experiments used LoRA for efficient full-layer fine-tuning and OneCycleLR scheduler to break optimization plateaus, while failed experiments showed that complex multi-scale fusion and progressive unfreezing (currently used) lead to diminishing returns. Therefore, I focus on model_design (replace progressive unfreezing with LoRA) and training_evaluation (use OneCycleLR and Focal Loss) to directly address root causes without deviating from the initial plan's direction. Data augmentation is kept but simplified (only synonym replacement, no punctuation swapping) based on successful historical setups.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current progressive unfreezing and multi-scale fusion (including PunctuationPatternEncoder, ArchaicVocabularyDetector, LayerAttention, and GatedCrossAttention) with LoRA applied to all query/value projection matrices in all 24 layers of DeBERTa-v3-large (rank=8, alpha=16, dropout=0.1). Keep only the classification head and remove all auxiliary modules to avoid overfitting. This allows simultaneous all-layer adaptation with only ~2M trainable parameters, directly addressing the underfitting caused by frozen backbone layers.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current cosine annealing scheduler (stuck at 1.71e-05) with OneCycleLR (max_lr=5e-4 for LoRA params, 1e-3 for head, pct_start=0.1, anneal_strategy='cos'), and replace label smoothing cross-entropy with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=class-balanced). Keep data augmentation as synonym replacement (30% of content words via WordNet) applied inside __getitem__. This provides proper learning rate dynamics and focuses on hard examples, breaking the validation plateau."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current solution with LoRA achieved validation logloss ~1.097, plateauing quickly. Reference solutions show that full fine-tuning of DeBERTa-v3-large (unfreezing all layers) yields much better performance (logloss ~0.16-0.20) for this task, as the dataset is small and task requires full model capacity. Additionally, using stratification in validation split and longer training with cosine annealing scheduler were common among top performers. The main limitations are: (1) LoRA restricts model capacity too much for this text classification task, and (2) the learning rate and scheduler are not optimal. Therefore, I will switch to full fine-tuning of the backbone, remove LoRA, increase batch size, and use a proper cosine annealing scheduler with warmup.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Remove LoRA modules entirely and unfreeze all backbone parameters of DeBERTa-v3-large (full fine-tuning). Keep only the classification head (3-layer MLP). This change directly addresses the underfitting issue caused by LoRA's limited capacity. The model will have ~430M trainable parameters, matching reference solutions that achieved logloss ~0.16-0.20.", "training_evaluation": "Increase batch size to 16 (from 8) to speed up training. Replace OneCycleLR with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=10, T_mult=2) to allow multiple annealing cycles. Reduce learning rate to 2e-5 for backbone and 2e-4 for head, with AdamW optimizer (weight_decay=0.01). Increase epochs to 50 with early stopping patience of 10. Remove Focal Loss and use standard cross-entropy as references show it works well with full fine-tuning. Use 5-fold stratified cross-validation for better generalization, averaging predictions."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current solution with LoRA achieved validation logloss ~1.097, plateauing quickly. Reference solutions show that full fine-tuning of DeBERTa-v3-large (unfreezing all layers) yields much better performance (logloss ~0.16-0.20) for this task, as the dataset is small and task requires full model capacity. Additionally, using stratification in validation split and longer training with cosine annealing scheduler were common among top performers. The main limitations are: (1) LoRA restricts model capacity too much for this text classification task, and (2) the learning rate and scheduler are not optimal. Therefore, I will switch to full fine-tuning of the backbone, remove LoRA, increase batch size, and use a proper cosine annealing scheduler with warmup.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Remove LoRA modules entirely and unfreeze all backbone parameters of DeBERTa-v3-large (full fine-tuning). Keep only the classification head (3-layer MLP). This change directly addresses the underfitting issue caused by LoRA's limited capacity. The model will have ~430M trainable parameters, matching reference solutions that achieved logloss ~0.16-0.20.",
    "training_evaluation": "Increase batch size to 16 (from 8) to speed up training. Replace OneCycleLR with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=10, T_mult=2) to allow multiple annealing cycles. Reduce learning rate to 2e-5 for backbone and 2e-4 for head, with AdamW optimizer (weight_decay=0.01). Increase epochs to 50 with early stopping patience of 10. Remove Focal Loss and use standard cross-entropy as references show it works well with full fine-tuning. Use 5-fold stratified cross-validation for better generalization, averaging predictions."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show the model achieves a validation log loss of ~0.0736 yet exhibits persistent volatility (oscillating between 0.07 and 0.48 in later epochs), confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that the model memorizes shallow patterns without robust stylistic generalization. The initial plan's proposed changes—stylometric feature injection, supervised contrastive loss, and targeted data augmentation—are well-aligned with successful historical experiments: the first experiment achieved strong performance using 5-fold CV and full fine-tuning of DeBERTa-v3-large; the second experiment improved from LoRA to full fine-tuning with proper scheduler. Conversely, the failed attempts (multi-scale fusion and progressive unfreezing, or two-phase training with focal loss) showed that complex architectural modifications or aggressive loss changes can destabilize training. Therefore, I select model_design to incorporate stylometric features and contrastive loss as modular additions, and training_evaluation to refine the augmentation strategy and adopt multi-fold ensemble for stability. These modules directly address the root causes while avoiding over-engineered changes that historically failed.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "1) Add a StylometricFeatureEncoder: a small feed-forward network (input 141 features -> hidden 256 -> output 128) that processes the scaled handcrafted features into a dense stylometric embedding. Concatenate this embedding with the [CLS] token from DeBERTa before the classifier head. 2) Add a supervised contrastive loss: during training, project the concatenated representation (CLS + stylometric) via a separate projection head (256 -> 128) and compute SupCon loss pulling same-author embeddings together and pushing different authors apart. Final loss = 0.7 * CrossEntropy + 0.3 * SupCon. This directly injects explicit stylistic signals and enforces representation clustering, as suggested by the initial plan and supported by the success of hybrid feature approaches in historical exps.", "training_evaluation": "1) Replace uniform synonym replacement with targeted augmentation: never replace archaic/horror keywords, increase prob to 40% for stop words, decrease to 15% for rare words, and add random punctuation perturbation (swap period for exclamation with 10% prob) to preserve author-specific tics. 2) Switch from a single train/val split to 5-fold stratified cross-validation with model averaging, as successful historical experiments show ensemble reduces variance and improves stability. 3) Keep the existing full fine-tuning, cosine annealing scheduler, early stopping patience of 10, and batch size 16; the augmentation refinement prevents overfitting to surface patterns while the ensemble smooths the volatile validation curve observed."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show the model achieves a validation log loss of ~0.0736 yet exhibits persistent volatility (oscillating between 0.07 and 0.48 in later epochs), confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that the model memorizes shallow patterns without robust stylistic generalization. The initial plan's proposed changes—stylometric feature injection, supervised contrastive loss, and targeted data augmentation—are well-aligned with successful historical experiments: the first experiment achieved strong performance using 5-fold CV and full fine-tuning of DeBERTa-v3-large; the second experiment improved from LoRA to full fine-tuning with proper scheduler. Conversely, the failed attempts (multi-scale fusion and progressive unfreezing, or two-phase training with focal loss) showed that complex architectural modifications or aggressive loss changes can destabilize training. Therefore, I select model_design to incorporate stylometric features and contrastive loss as modular additions, and training_evaluation to refine the augmentation strategy and adopt multi-fold ensemble for stability. These modules directly address the root causes while avoiding over-engineered changes that historically failed.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "1) Add a StylometricFeatureEncoder: a small feed-forward network (input 141 features -> hidden 256 -> output 128) that processes the scaled handcrafted features into a dense stylometric embedding. Concatenate this embedding with the [CLS] token from DeBERTa before the classifier head. 2) Add a supervised contrastive loss: during training, project the concatenated representation (CLS + stylometric) via a separate projection head (256 -> 128) and compute SupCon loss pulling same-author embeddings together and pushing different authors apart. Final loss = 0.7 * CrossEntropy + 0.3 * SupCon. This directly injects explicit stylistic signals and enforces representation clustering, as suggested by the initial plan and supported by the success of hybrid feature approaches in historical exps.",
    "training_evaluation": "1) Replace uniform synonym replacement with targeted augmentation: never replace archaic/horror keywords, increase prob to 40% for stop words, decrease to 15% for rare words, and add random punctuation perturbation (swap period for exclamation with 10% prob) to preserve author-specific tics. 2) Switch from a single train/val split to 5-fold stratified cross-validation with model averaging, as successful historical experiments show ensemble reduces variance and improves stability. 3) Keep the existing full fine-tuning, cosine annealing scheduler, early stopping patience of 10, and batch size 16; the augmentation refinement prevents overfitting to surface patterns while the ensemble smooths the volatile validation curve observed."
  }
}"}
